\fs36 \cf0 1. Word Pivot\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Formula:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 A(word) \u8594  B(same word, new meaning)\

\f0\b Plain English:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 One word has two meanings. The joke switches between them.\

\f0\b Examples:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 \'93I used to be a banker, but I lost interest.\'94\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A = interest (curiosity)\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 B = interest (money)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93This room is too intense.\'94\uc0\u8232 \'93No, it\'92s just in tents.\'94\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A = intense\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 B = in tents\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Phrase Pivot\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Formula:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 A(phrase) \u8594  B(same phrase, new meaning)\

\f0\b Plain English:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 A full phrase (often an idiom) is reinterpreted.\

\f0\b Example:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 \'93He broke my heart, so I went to the doctor.\'94\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A = broke my heart (emotional)\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 B = broke my heart (physical)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. Structure-Based Pun\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Formula:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 A(structure) \u8594  B(same structure, new interpretation)\

\f0\b Plain English:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 The sentence format stays the same, but key parts change meaning.\

\f0\b Examples:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 \'93What do you call a fish with no eyes?\'94\u8232 \'93Fsh.\'94\
\'93Why did the math book look sad?\'94\uc0\u8232 \'93Because it had too many problems.\'94\
\'93Time flies like an arrow. Fruit flies like a banana.\'94\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 flies / like change meaning across the same structure\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Full Reinterpretation (Narrative Flip)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Formula:
